  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY through FRIDAY/...\nHemispheric pattern amplification driven by an unusually early \nseason sudden stratospheric warming (SSW) event will be the main \nstory for the latter half of next week with large ensemble \nforecast spread persisting. For much of the week, northern stream \njet energy will settle over southern Canada while weak low \namplitude waves in the southern stream remain sequestered over \nnorthern Mexico. As a result, subsident ridging with nearly steady\nmidtropospheric heights will define the pattern over the SW Conus\nyielding high confidence of a very tranquil, near persistence \nforecast carrying on from early in the week. By the end of the \nweek, effects from disruption of the polar vortex will be \nmanifested in poleward retrogressive ridging while deep negative \nheight anomalies descending equatorward over the NE Conus and East\nPacific. However, the specifics regarding depth, positioning, and\nevolution of features within the greater synoptic pattern remain \nhighly uncertain. Recent operational models peel a notable \nshortwave from the east Pacific trough towards the SW \nConus/northern Mexico late in the week, however this type of \nsolution is not consistent among NAEFS and EPS members. Given the \namplification phase, wide ensemble spread and large run-to-run \nvariations are likely the next several days resulting in low \nforecast confidence for next weekend.",
